# Source-order additions; existing gup5 rows keep their stable IDs.
J='Janaka (Mithilā king in65)';VC='Virocanā (Triśiras wife in65–66)';TR='Triśiras (poor Brahmin in65–66)';BG='Gaṇeśa old Brahmin form in65–66'
S1='gup5-story-janaka-test';S2='gup5-story-asraya-weighing'
e,v=ev(65,'1–6','Kauṇḍinya recounts Nārada visiting Gaṇeśa after many days. The sage bows, praises the fruitful sight and stands with joined hands. Gaṇeśa holds his hand and seats him. Nārada says he came to report an astonishing matter and would depart after bowing. Gaṇeśa asks him to describe everything before returning to his hermitage.',[(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Nārada',S),('Gaṇeśa',D)],story=S1,outcome='Nārada receives an explicit invitation to tell the report.')
e,v=ev(65,'7–16','Nārada reports Janaka of Mithilā as proud, generous and learned in Vedas and Vedāṅgas: he continually feeds and honours Brahmins with ornaments, clothing and gifts, gives wealth to poor, blind and destitute people, and grants petitions. Nārada wonders whether Gaṇeśa’s satisfaction accounts for undiminished wealth. On visiting, Janaka mocks him through pride in Brahman knowledge. Nārada praises Gaṇeśa as giver of the king’s devotional desires, but Janaka claims to be lord, giver, enjoyer, protector, maker, cause and instrument, with none other in three worlds. Angered, Nārada says God alone makes the world, challenges the king’s apparent religious display, and promises evidence soon.',[('Nārada',S),('Gaṇeśa',D),(J,F),('Mithilā',L),('Brahmins honoured by Janaka','FAMILY'),('Janaka petitioners','FAMILY')],story=S1,outcome='The king’s attributed claims and Nārada’s challenge motivate the later test.')
val(J,'attributed_self_claim','Lord, giver, enjoyer, protector, maker, cause and instrument of the three worlds65.13–14; a claim by Janaka, not narrator certification.',e)
e,v=ev(65,'17–28','Having heard the report, Gaṇeśa honours Nārada with arghya, ornaments, flowers and sandal. With permission Nārada goes to Viṣṇu in Vaikuṇṭha. Gaṇeśa comes to Mithilā to test the king’s devotion, wearing a repulsive old Brahmin form with bleeding wounds, flies and missing teeth. He stumbles, faints and falls amid children; people cover their noses and spit. At the royal gate he asks to be announced as an old, hungry Brahmin guest desiring a meal. Janaka orders his admission, observes his filthy clothes, trembling, bleeding and exhaustion, suspects a divine test, and resolves to satisfy him if merit allows.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),('Nārada',S),('Viṣṇu',D),('Vaikuṇṭha',L),('Mithilā',L),(J,F),(BG,'MANIFESTATION'),('Janaka gatekeepers','FAMILY'),('Mithilā townspeople','FAMILY')],story=S1,outcome='The narrator identifies the disguised deity; Janaka separately conjectures a divine guest.')
rel(BG,'manifestation_of','Gaṇeśa',e)
e,v=ev(65,'29–35','The guest says he came after hearing Janaka’s fame and asks food until fully satisfied, promising the merit of one hundred sacrifices. Janaka takes him inside, honours him and serves tasty food. The guest consumes the entire portion in one mouthful, then a mountain-sized portion in one mouthful, then prepared food sufficient for ten thousand. Rice is put to cook in innumerable vessels and he eats every finished batch.',[(BG,'MANIFESTATION'),('Gaṇeśa',D),(J,F)],story=S1,outcome='Successive immense servings fail to satisfy the guest.')
power(J,e,v,mechanism='The guest promises merit equal to one hundred sacrifices.',trigger='Feed the guest until he is satisfied.',notes='A conditional promise spoken by the disguised deity; Janaka does not accomplish its stated satisfaction condition in this account.')
power('Gaṇeśa',e,v,mechanism='The old Brahmin manifestation consumes whole and mountain-sized portions in one mouthful and food sufficient for ten thousand without satisfaction.',notes='Specific test action; no remembered universal appetite claim added.')
e,v=ev(65,'36–45','Some advisers conjecture that the guest is a Rākṣasa and gifts to such beings give no merit; others say even the three worlds could not satisfy him and urge offering grain. All palace, city and village grain is gathered and consumed, still without satisfaction. Messengers report none remains; Janaka lowers his head. The guest departs house to house asking food. Residents explain their grain was collected by the king and already eaten by him. He questions how he can leave unsatisfied after hearing Janaka’s unparalleled giving fame. Amid silence he finds the Brahmin home of Virocanā and Triśiras, enters like its owner, and sees no equipment or metal vessels.',[(BG,'MANIFESTATION'),('Gaṇeśa',D),(J,F),('Janaka advisers','FAMILY'),('Janaka messengers','FAMILY'),('Mithilā townspeople','FAMILY'),(VC,F),(TR,S),('Virocanā–Triśiras home',L)],story=S1,outcome='Janaka’s resources are exhausted; the test continues in a poor couple’s home.')
e,v=ev(66,'1–9','The couple sits on bare earth, with sky as covering, avoiding contact with metals. They eat unasked food, use water for their rites and maintain purity. Amid flies and mosquitoes their Gaṇeśa image is worshipped with flowers and leaves in exclusive devotion. The disguised guest says Janaka did not satisfy him and ostentatious action cannot preserve sattva; he asks anything in their house that could satisfy him. The couple wonders how they, poor people, can feed one whom an emperor could not, comparing a drop with rivers unable to fill the ocean. He says even a small devotional gift greatly satisfies him; abundant gifts without devotion or given for display are fruitless.',[(VC,F),(TR,S),(BG,'MANIFESTATION'),('Gaṇeśa',D),(J,F),('Virocanā–Triśiras home',L)],story=S1,outcome='The guest explains why devotional giving differs from abundance without devotion.')
rel(VC,'spouse_of',TR,e,'MARITAL');rel(TR,'worships','Gaṇeśa',e);rel(VC,'worships','Gaṇeśa',e)
e,v=ev(66,'10–16','The couple swears nothing remains except one dūrvā sprout left from the morning Gaṇeśa worship. At the guest’s request Virocanā offers that single sprout with devotion, imagining rice, pāyasa, varied cooked dishes, accompaniments and lickable or suckable foods upon it. He joyfully eats it; his stomach-fire immediately subsides, he gains complete satisfaction and embraces Triśiras.',[(VC,F),(TR,S),(BG,'MANIFESTATION'),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Dūrvā',C)],story=S1,outcome='One actually offered sprout accompanied by imagined foods satisfies the guest whom abundant actual food did not satisfy.')
power('Gaṇeśa',e,v,mechanism='The single devoted dūrvā offering immediately quiets the guest’s stomach-fire and fully satisfies him.',trigger='Virocanā’s actual one-sprout offering with devotional imagined foods.',notes='The imagined dishes are not falsely counted as physical food supplied by the couple.')
e,v=ev(66,'17–22','Gaṇeśa abandons the repulsive disguise and appears four-armed, lotus-eyed and trunk-bearing, holding lotus, axe, rosary and tusk, wearing costly crown, earrings, divine clothing and scents. He invites the couple to choose their desired boon. They ask for firm devotion wherever they are born, or liberation from the difficult ocean of transmigration, wanting nothing else. He assents with tathā, joyfully embraces Triśiras again and vanishes.',[('Gaṇeśa',D),(VC,F),(TR,S),('Lotus held in66',C),('Gaṇeśa axe in66',W),('Gaṇeśa rosary in66',C),('Gaṇeśa tusk held in66',W),('Virocanā–Triśiras couple','FAMILY')],story=S1,outcome='The deity explicitly assents and withdraws; no later death or selected alternative is invented.')
val('Gaṇeśa','manifest_form_66','Four arms holding lotus, axe, rosary and tusk66.17–18.',e)
power('Virocanā–Triśiras couple',e,v,typ='BOON',grant='Gaṇeśa',requested='Firm devotion to Gaṇeśa wherever they are born, OR liberation from transmigration; no other desire.',granted='Explicit assent tathā to the couple’s request.',notes='The text does not select between the alternatives or narrate their subsequent death/transport; assent and withdrawal close this bounded test.')
e,v=ev(66,'23–25','Kauṇḍinya tells Aśrayā that this is why he offers dūrvā: the deity who remained unsatisfied by innumerable foods gained full satisfaction through one sprout. He states that hearing the offering’s greatness gives all wishes; devoted hearing or telling brings sons, wealth and enjoyment here and hereafter, Gaṇeśa devotion, and liberation for a desireless person.',[(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Dūrvā',C),('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers','FAMILY')],story=S1,outcome='Kauṇḍinya applies the completed test account to his own practice.')
power('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers',e,v,mechanism='All wishes, sons, wealth, enjoyment here and hereafter, and Gaṇeśa devotion; liberation for a desireless person.',trigger='Devotionally hear or tell this history.',restrictions='The liberation promise specifically describes a desireless person.')
e,v=ev(66,'26–31','The rescuing Gaṇas say Aśrayā still doubts. Knowing her heart, Kauṇḍinya instructs her to take one dūrvā to Indra and convey his blessing and request: obtain gold equal in weight to this sprout, neither less nor more, and bring it back. She accepts the instruction and takes the sprout to Indra.',[(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Indra',D),('Dūrvā',C),('Gaṇeśa-shaped rescuing Gaṇas in62','FAMILY')],story=S2,outcome='An exact equal-weight request opens a second demonstration, distinct from a request for abundant wealth.')
e,v=ev(67,'1–11','Aśrayā asks Indra for gold at her husband’s instruction. Indra says a message could have sufficed for him to send gold; she insists on exactly the sprout’s weight, neither less nor more. He sends a divine messenger with her to Kubera, requesting the exact measure. Kubera laughs at the sage, Indra and Aśrayā for seeking so little, and gives much gold. She refuses it from fear of her husband and doubt about unequal weight. A goldsmith’s scale and then a merchant’s scale fail to balance the dūrvā.',[(AŚ,F),(K,S),('Indra',D),('Kubera',D),('Indra messenger for Aśrayā',F),('Dūrvā',C),('Kubera palace in67',L)],story=S2,outcome='Offering abundant gold does not satisfy the stated equal-weight condition.')
e,v=ev(67,'12–21','An oilman’s scale also fails. A pot is attached and gold placed on one side, while the dūrvā’s pan descends. Kubera adds more gold, then all treasury wealth like a mountain; it still does not equal the sprout. He asks his unnamed devoted wife to climb into the pot and proposes to follow if that fails. She enters; it remains unequal. He adds the entire city and himself. Hearing from the messenger, Indra comes on an elephant and enters with his wealth, yet the sprout does not rise. Troubled, he invokes Viṣṇu and Śiva, who arrive with their cities and mount the pot; still the sprout does not rise.',[('Kubera',D),('Kubera unnamed wife in67',F),('Indra',D),('Indra elephant in67','VAHANA'),('Viṣṇu',D),('Śiva',D),('Dūrvā',C)],story=S2,outcome='The mounting sequence adds gold, treasury, wife, city, Kubera, Indra with wealth, and Viṣṇu/Śiva with cities without raising the sprout.')
rel('Kubera unnamed wife in67','spouse_of','Kubera',e,'MARITAL')
power('Dūrvā',e,v,mechanism='The devotedly offered sprout outweighs the enumerated gold, wealth, divine persons and cities in the narrated trial.',notes='No numerical weight or universal mass equation is invented;67.40’s wording is separately flagged against this sequence.')
e,v=ev(67,'22–31','Śiva, Viṣṇu and Kubera descend. With Varuṇa, Indra, Fire, Maruts, gods, divine sages, Siddhas, Vidyādharas and Uragas, they go to Kauṇḍinya at day’s end. They bow and say his sight removes sin and earlier merit promises future good; his wife has taken all their strength. They confess they do not know the sprout’s greatness: even three worlds did not equal one offered with devotion upon Gaṇeśa’s head; sacrifices, gifts, vows, tapas and study do not match it, nor can they fully know the sage’s exclusive devotion, japa and tapas. All worship Gaṇeśa and the sage with their wives, hymn, dance and sing. The hymn names Brahmā, Hari, Śiva, Indra, Maruts, Fire, Vivasvān, Yama, Śeṣa, Varuṇa, Moon, Aśvins, the lord of speech, Garuḍa, Yakṣa king and Aṅgiras as unable to fully know the deity. Having pleased him, they obtain the sage’s permission and return home.',[('Śiva',D),('Viṣṇu',D),('Kubera',D),('Varuṇa',D),('Indra',D),('Fire',D),('Maruts',C),('Gods',C),('Divine sages','FAMILY'),('Siddhas',C),('Vidyādharas',C),('Uragas',C),(K,S),(AŚ,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Brahmā',D),('Vivasvān in67 hymn',D),('Yama',D),('Śeṣa',D),('Moon',D),('Aśvins',D),('Lord of speech in67 hymn','EPITHET'),('Garuḍa',D),('Yakṣa king in67 hymn','EPITHET'),('Aṅgiras',S),('Visiting gods’ wives in67','FAMILY')],story=S2,outcome='The gods acknowledge the experiment’s failure, praise the devotion, worship and depart.')
e,v=ev(67,'32–39','Aśrayā now knows dūrvā’s greatness, trusts her husband’s words, worships Vighneśvara with dūrvā and bows to Kauṇḍinya. She reproaches her own doubt, praises his knowledge and compassion and asks pardon while taking refuge. The couple rises at dawn, collects dūrvā, bathes and offers it with exclusive devotion. They worship morning and evening continuously, relinquishing sacrifice, vow and gifts. Knowing this, Gaṇeśa compassionately brings them to his own abode.',[(AŚ,F),(K,S),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Dūrvā',C)],story=S2,outcome='Doubt resolves, sustained joint worship follows, and the couple actually reaches Gaṇeśa’s abode.')
power(K,e,v,grant='Gaṇeśa',mechanism='Gaṇeśa compassionately conveys Kauṇḍinya and Aśrayā to his abode.',trigger='Their continuous exclusive morning/evening dūrvā worship after the trial.',notes='Actual joint transport, not merely prospective liberation advice.')
power(AŚ,e,v,grant='Gaṇeśa',mechanism='Gaṇeśa compassionately conveys Aśrayā and Kauṇḍinya to his abode.',trigger='Their continuous exclusive morning/evening dūrvā worship after the trial.')
e,v=ev(67,'40–46','The Gaṇas state no Śeṣa, Brahmā, Hari or Īśvara can exhaust dūrvā’s greatness. Verse40 says the three worlds became equal by its leaf on a scale, while the preceding trial and26 say they did not equal the sprout; these formulations are retained without reconciliation. Remembering dūrvā removes threefold sin because it also recalls Gaṇeśa. Hearing, recounting or meditating the Cintāmaṇi-site greatness brings enjoyment and liberation. They explain that this caused the rescue vimāna for the three: dūrvā fell from the bull/donkey mouths and the woman’s warming grass through strong wind onto Gaṇeśa. Pleased, he purified them and gave his own presence. Even its scent satisfies him, incidentally or with devotion, still more offering it upon his head.',[('Gaṇeśa-shaped rescuing Gaṇas in62','FAMILY'),('Temple yogins in62','FAMILY'),('Śeṣa',D),('Brahmā',D),('Viṣṇu',D),('Śiva',D),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Dūrvā',C),('Sulabha (kṣatriya in62)',F),('Madhusūdana (Brahmin visitor in62)',S),('Sumudrā (Sulabha wife)',F),('Cintāmaṇi site in dūrvā frame',L),('Dūrvā-remembrance practitioners','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='The Gaṇas explicitly answer the yogins’ rescue question; the scale wording remains unresolved.')
power('Dūrvā-remembrance practitioners',e,v,mechanism='Threefold sin-removal through remembering dūrvā and thereby the deity.',trigger='Remember dūrvā.')
power('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers',e,v,mechanism='Enjoyment and liberation from the narrated Cintāmaṇi-site glory.',trigger='Hearing, recounting or meditating this site glory.')
u=note_evidence(67,'26,40','67.26 says even the three worlds did not obtain equality with one dūrvā sprout;67.40 prints trailokyaṃ tulayā yasyāḥ patreṇaivābhavat samam, describing equality by its leaf. Exact compatibility of these source-local formulations remains unresolved. Neither passage is silently emended.','NEEDS_REVIEW')
variant('67-scale-formulations','The failure-to-balance trial and26 versus samam equality wording40 require grammatical/edition review; narrative sequence and actual couple transport remain secure.',u)
next(p for p in d['passages']if p['id']=='gup5-p-67-40-46')['extraction_status']='PARTIAL'
e,v=ev(67,'47–56','Brahmā says the king and sages hear the messenger’s unprecedented dūrvā account. After bathing, they and servants worship Vināyaka with sprouts. They obtain radiant divine bodies, hear divine music, board a fine vimāna in divine clothes and scents, and reach Vaināyaka’s abode, some bearing his form. Townspeople who come to the celebration each offer twenty-one dūrvā, enjoy their allotted pleasures and eventually reach Gaṇeśa’s place; the vimāna moves upward through this merit. The discourse instructs devotees to worship with dūrvā, condemns deliberate omission through caṇḍāla and hell language, says seeing a dūrvā worshipper purifies another sinner, and permits one sprout when many are unavailable, multiplying worship a crore-fold.',[('Brahmā',D),('King in rescued-trio frame67',F),('Temple yogins in62','FAMILY'),('Rescued-trio frame servants in67','FAMILY'),('Festival townspeople in62','FAMILY'),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Trio-rescue vimāna in62','VAHANA'),('Dūrvā-offering practitioners','FAMILY'),('Dūrvā-worshipper onlookers','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='The yogins/king/servants actually gain divine bodies and abode; townspeople’s later destination is also narrated. Disparaging prescriptions are attributed to the source.')
power('Dūrvā-offering practitioners',e,v,mechanism='One sprout when many are unavailable gives worship a crore-fold effect.',trigger='Offer one dūrvā when many cannot be obtained.',restrictions='Discourse condemns deliberate omission of dūrvā worship and assigns hells; source-attributed prescription.')
power('Dūrvā-worshipper onlookers',e,v,mechanism='Another sinner becomes purified upon seeing a dūrvā worshipper.',trigger='See the person who worships Gaṇeśa with dūrvā.')
e,v=ev(67,'57–59','Brahmā closes the dūrvā history to the king, calling hearing it sin-destroying, forbidding its telling to someone of wicked understanding and prescribing communication to a beloved son. Indra says the father hears this excellent account from Brahmā, rejoices, bows and returns to his own place with permission and wonder.',[('Brahmā',D),('Kṛtavīrya unnamed father',F),('Indra',D),('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers','FAMILY')],story='gup5-story-durva-frame',outcome='The nested dūrvā teaching closes; the father returns before the next dream-delivery account.')
power('Dūrvā-account readers/hearers',e,v,mechanism='Sin-destruction from hearing this dūrvā history.',trigger='Hear this history.',restrictions='Do not recount it to wicked-minded people; communicate it to a beloved son67.57.')
S3='gup5-story-krtavirya-dream';KV='Kṛtavīrya';KF='Kṛtavīrya unnamed father';BOOK='Sankaṣṭa vow book delivered in68';RIT='Sankaṣṭa-caturthī practitioners';S4='gup5-story-sankasta-procedure';S5='gup5-story-vow-exempla'
e,v=ev(68,'1–12','Śūrasena asks Indra what Kṛtavīrya’s father did next. Indra says the son-seeking king anxiously considers childlessness after hearing the Sankaṣṭa and dūrvā accounts. Verse3 calls the dreamer Kārtavīrya, while the waking king is explicitly Kṛtavīrya in13; the name variation is retained. He dreams of his father. They embrace, unable to speak through emotion. The father takes his hand, seats him and says Nārada reported his efforts for a son, prompting the father’s visit to Brahmā. Brahmā prescribed Sankaṣṭa-caturthī and promised offspring to the royal son once the vow removes sin. The father says he recorded the instructions, gives the book, and tells him to practice for a year so Siddhivināyaka will be pleased and a son will result.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Indra',D),(KV,F),(KF,F),('Nārada',S),('Brahmā',D),('Gaṇeśa',D),(BOOK,T)],story=S3,outcome='The father delivers a book and prospective remedy in the dream; the printed dreamer name remains under review.')
power(KV,e,v,mechanism='Brahmā predicts offspring for the father’s royal son after the vow and removal of sin; father predicts pleased Siddhivināyaka and son after one year.',trigger='Proper Sankaṣṭa-caturthī practice for a year with sin-removal.',notes='Reported prospective assurance, not a newly narrated Gaṇeśa appearance or actual sonbirth.')
e,v=ev(68,'13–21','The father vanishes; the king, explicitly named Kṛtavīrya, awakens and sees the book in his hand. Recalling the dream, he weeps from sorrow at separation and joy at obtaining the book. His ministers gather, urge care and ask the cause, saying his grief affects them. He explains the father’s appearance, gift, immediate disappearance and instruction to practice for a son, and says wonder, joy and sorrow arise because the book remains in his hand after waking.',[(KV,F),(KF,F),('Kṛtavīrya ministers','FAMILY'),(BOOK,T)],story=S3,outcome='Actual waking possession of the book is narrated separately from the ministers’ interpretation.')
rel(KF,'father_of',KV,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_FATHER')
val(BOOK,'narrated_delivery','Given by the father in a dream and physically present in Kṛtavīrya’s hand after waking68.13–21.',e)
e,v=ev(68,'22–32','The ministers infer that Gaṇeśa, father of all, came as the king’s father and gave the remedy, arguing the book and true dream otherwise lack an explanation. Indra says the king steadies himself, summons scholars and friends, and asks the book’s meaning. The Brahmins identify a Brahmā–Kṛtavīrya dialogue, Sankaṣṭa-caturthī, detailed moonrise worship, Aṅgāraka-fourth glory, arghya mantras for tithi, Gaṇeśa and Moon, feeding and honouring twenty-one Brahmins with gifts, and distinct ordinary/white-dūrvā benefits. They call it fortunate and beneficial, removing distress by hearing and remembering. King and public rejoice and he honours them.',[('Kṛtavīrya ministers','FAMILY'),(KV,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Brahmā',D),('Kṛtavīrya interpreting scholars','FAMILY'),('Kṛtavīrya summoned friends','FAMILY'),('Kṛtavīrya assembled public','FAMILY'),(BOOK,T),(RIT,'FAMILY')],story=S3,outcome='The divine father-form remains an attributed ministerial inference; scholarly interpretation prepares the king’s actual practice.')
val('Kṛtavīrya ministers','attributed_inference','The dream father was pleased Gaṇeśa in father-form68.22–23; narrator identity is not independently established.',e)
power(RIT,e,v,mechanism='Distress-removal from hearing and remembering the vow as explained by scholars.',trigger='Hear or remember the Sankaṣṭa vow account.',notes='Source-attributed scholarly statement68.31.')
e,v=ev(68,'33–35','Kṛtavīrya gives the scholars abundant clothing, ornaments, jewels, wealth and grain. He summons Atri, explicitly guru of his own family. At an auspicious time he properly honours Atri and Vināyaka and receives the one-syllable vidyā. Controlling senses, meditating Gaṇeśa and japping with exclusive devotion, he actually practices the Sankaṣṭa vow to please Gaṇanātha and obtain a son.',[(KV,F),('Atri',S),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Kṛtavīrya interpreting scholars','FAMILY'),('Single-syllable Gaṇeśa mantra',T)],story=S3,outcome='Family guru instruction and actual observance follow; no child is born in this chapter.')
rel('Atri','family_guru_of',KV,e);rel('Atri','teaches_mantra_to',KV,e);rel(KV,'worships','Gaṇeśa',e)
u=note_evidence(68,'3,13,18','The son-seeking dreamer is printed kārtavīryaḥ in68.3; the waking and speaking king is kṛtavīryaḥ in13 and18. Exact intended consistency of these forms awaits collation; no separate father/son actor mapping is forced from the variant.','NEEDS_REVIEW')
variant('68-dreamer-name','Kārtavīrya68.3 versus Kṛtavīrya68.13/18, within one dream/waking sequence. Stable Kṛtavīrya actor used where explicitly named; anomalous3 not silently emended.',u)
# Chapter69 is prescribed worship, not a new enactment of every formula’s cosmic genealogy.
e,v=ev(69,'1–9','Śūrasena asks Indra how Brahmā taught Kṛtavīrya the successful Sankaṣṭa vow. Indra recounts the father approaching Brahmā in Satyaloka and asking what people in distress, anxiety, separation from friends, difficult undertakings, or seeking wealth, sons and good fortune should do. Brahmā prescribes the wish-accomplishing vow: daytime bathing with herbs and white sesame, proper intention and Gaṇeśa meditation, and devotional worship with Āgama mantras on dark fourth at night’s moonrise. The king asks the exact worship method.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Indra',D),(KF,F),(KV,F),('Brahmā',D),('Satyaloka',L),('Gaṇeśa',D),(RIT,'FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='Brahmā answers a general remedy request and specifies time, preparation and ritual authority.')
power(RIT,e,v,mechanism='The prescribed vow gives intended accomplishment.',trigger='Perform the described Sankaṣṭa vow with intention, meditation and devotional worship at dark-fourth moonrise.',notes='Prescriptive efficacy, not a personal boon granted after an appearance.')
e,v=ev(69,'10–17','After daily rites at moonrise, prepare a clean cow-dung-coated place with a pavilion and honour Gaṇeśa’s seat with kuṅkuma and rice. Set a kalaśa with five gems and a gold vessel; silver, copper or bamboo is permitted when unavailable, and cloth according to ability. Draw the Āgama-prescribed yantra and install a well-formed gold image. Meditate one-tusked, huge, molten-gold-coloured, large-bellied, wide-eyed Gaṇeśa with fire-like eyes, riding a mouse, fanned by Gaṇas, with Śeṣa as sacred thread. Invoke him to remove distress and remain present until the vow ends.',[('Brahmā',D),(KF,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gaṇeśa ritual mouse in69','VAHANA'),('Gaṇas fanning ritual image in69','FAMILY'),('Śeṣa',D),('Sankaṣṭa ritual Gaṇeśa image',C),('Sankaṣṭa ritual yantra',C),(RIT,'FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='The image/mount/thread are meditation instructions; no actual travel or new birth is inferred.')
val('Sankaṣṭa ritual Gaṇeśa image','prescribed_form','One tusk, huge gold-coloured form, large belly, broad fire-like eyes, mouse mount, Gaṇas fanning, Śeṣa sacred thread69.15–16.',e)
e,v=ev(69,'18–29','The prescribed offerings proceed through seat, foot-water, gem-and-fruit arghya, sipping water from Gaṅgā and other tīrthas, pañcāmṛta of milk/curd/ghee/sugar/honey, bathing with Narmadā, Candrabhāgā and Gaṅgā-confluence waters, two garments, sacred thread, sandal, ghee/kuṅkuma rice, campaka/mallikā/dūrvā and other flowers, and fragrant incense. The formulas repeatedly ask acceptance and distress-removal, addressing Gaṇeśa as Gaṇādhīśa, Umāputra, Modakapriya, Lambodara, Ibhavaktra, Gaṇādhyakṣa, Vināyaka, Paraśudhārin, Īśaputra, Mūṣakavāhana, Mahākāya and Dhūmraketu. Parenthetical Puruṣasūkta alternatives accompany many services.',[('Brahmā',D),(KF,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Gaṅgā',D),('Narmadā',L),('Candrabhāgā ritual-water river',L),('Dūrvā',C),('Puruṣasūkta ritual alternatives in69',T),(RIT,'FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='The service formulas supply petitions and source-specific epithets; they are not new narrated biological scenes.')
